mergence of metastable states that hinder convergence of the message-passing algorithm. These instabilities are characterized by analyzing the Hessian of the free energy and identifying critical points where local minima become unstable. Numerical Implementation and Validation The proposed algorithms were implemented in Python using optimized libraries for linear algebra and statistical sampling. To validate their performance, we conducted extensive numerical experiments on synthetic datasets generated under varying levels of noise, sparsity, and measurement rates \( \alpha = M/N \). For each experiment, we evaluated the mean squared error (MSE) between the true signal \( \mathbf{x}^0 \) and its reconstruction \( \hat{\mathbf{x}} \). Additionally, the theoretical predictions of phase transitions were compared against empirical results. Specifically, the predicted thresholds for successful inference were validated by observing sharp changes in the reconstruction MSE as the system parameters crossed critical values. This agreement confirms the accuracy of the replica analysis in predicting algorithmic performance. Applications to Real-World Problems The applicability of the methods was further demonstrated in three specific domains: (i) community detection in stochastic block models, where the posterior \( P(\mathbf{x}) \) encodes cluster memberships; (ii) decoding of superposition codes, where the signal \( \mathbf{x} \) represents a sparse codebook; and (iii) imaging using an innovative sensing architecture, where the bilinear structure arises from unknown calibration factors in the sensing matrix. For each application, we adapted the generic message-passing framework to exploit problem-specific symmetries and priors, enabling efficient and accurate inference. In summary, the methodological contributions of this work lie in the development, theoretical analysis, and empirical validation of message-passing algorithms for high-dimensional linear and bilinear inference problems. The integration of Bayesian inference, statistical physics, and numerical experimentation provides a comprehensive toolkit for addressing diverse challenges in signal processing and related fields. --- References [1] Pearl, J. Probabilistic reasoning in intelligent systems. Morgan Kaufmann, 1988. [2] Mézard, M., Parisi, G., & Virasoro, M. A. Spin Glass Theory and Beyond. World Scientific, 1987. [3] Nishimori, H. Statistical Physics of Spin Glasses and Information Processing. Oxford University Press, 2001. [4] Yedidia, J. S., Freeman, W. T., & Weiss, Y. Understanding belief propagation and its generalizations. IJCAI, 2001. [5] Donoho, D. L., Maleki, A., & Montanari, A. Message-passing algorithms for compressed sensing. PNAS, 2009. [6] Zdeborová, L., & Krzakala, F. Statistical physics of inference: Thresholds and algorithms. Advances in Physics, 2016. [7] Kabashima, Y., et al. Phase transitions in compressed sensing. Physical Review E,
